WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.114
Now the Leadership Mosaic deck.

00:00:02.114 --> 00:00:06.818
The framework pages show the same
twelve tiles as the Culture Mosaic.

00:00:06.818 --> 00:00:12.273
The item-detail slides show a row each
for Self, Boss, Peers and Direct Reports,

00:00:12.273 --> 00:00:14.114
with the grey typical band.

00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:17.045
Look at where Self sits
relative to others.

00:00:17.045 --> 00:00:21.546
The dimension heatmaps show each
item's percentile by rater group.

00:00:21.546 --> 00:00:26.386
The comparison diamonds and the grand
comparison heatmap show the whole

00:00:26.386 --> 00:00:31.705
self-versus-others picture, and the grand
heatmap is the best single slide for

00:00:31.705 --> 00:00:33.136
spotting blind spots.

00:00:33.136 --> 00:00:38.046
Then come the comment summaries per
rater group, with a sentiment label.

00:00:38.046 --> 00:00:41.386
Remember, there's no
dedicated blind-spots slide.

00:00:41.386 --> 00:00:44.591
You find them yourself,
and that's what's next.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:50.799
The heart of a 360 is the gap between how
leaders see themselves and how others see

00:00:50.799 --> 00:00:51.142
them.

00:00:51.142 --> 00:00:53.408
The gap is Self minus All Others.

00:00:53.408 --> 00:00:58.215
The Mosaic report engine treats a
gap of one point or more on an item,

00:00:58.215 --> 00:01:03.159
or half a point or more on a tile or
dimension, as big enough to matter.

00:01:03.159 --> 00:01:04.944
That gives three patterns.

00:01:04.944 --> 00:01:09.888
A blind spot is where others rate the
leader materially lower than Self.

00:01:09.888 --> 00:01:14.557
A hidden strength is where others
rate the leader materially higher.

00:01:14.557 --> 00:01:17.510
And agreement is a gap
below the threshold.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:22.934
For Jordan Reyes, Change Ready is a blind
spot: six point two from Self against

00:01:22.934 --> 00:01:24.857
four point four from others.

00:01:24.857 --> 00:01:28.221
Decisions is a hidden
strength: five against six.

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.938
And Inspire is agreement.

00:01:29.938 --> 00:01:33.783
With the leader, "blind spot"
can sound like a judgment,

00:01:33.783 --> 00:01:37.972
so try "a place where others'
experience differs from yours".

00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:39.108
Your turn.

00:01:39.108 --> 00:01:44.111
Drag each of Jordan's results into the
right bin: blind spot, hidden strength,

00:01:44.111 --> 00:01:44.945
or agreement.

00:01:45.433 --> 00:01:49.714
Sometimes the most interesting
finding is others versus others.

00:01:49.714 --> 00:01:55.149
On Empowered Teams, Jordan's boss gives
five point five, peers four point eight,

00:01:55.149 --> 00:01:57.528
and direct reports three point six.

00:01:57.528 --> 00:02:00.313
Different groups see different behaviour.

00:02:00.313 --> 00:02:06.021
The boss sees how Jordan manages up, and
direct reports see how Jordan manages down.

00:02:06.021 --> 00:02:10.777
A wide spread is common, and it's a
powerful development conversation.

00:02:10.777 --> 00:02:12.272
Don't average it away.

00:02:12.272 --> 00:02:15.873
Remember that bosses are
named, up to two, by design.

00:02:15.873 --> 00:02:20.969
And Jordan's two "Other" raters were
folded out and left out of All Others,

00:02:20.969 --> 00:02:22.939
with their comments withheld.

00:02:22.939 --> 00:02:25.453
Never speculate about what they said.

00:02:25.967 --> 00:02:30.221
In a Team Report, the numbers
mean slightly different things.

00:02:30.221 --> 00:02:34.546
The bars are the team mean, where
every leader counts equally.

00:02:34.546 --> 00:02:39.149
The band is the range across leaders,
not the pool's typical band.

00:02:39.149 --> 00:02:43.334
Percentiles are the average of
the leaders' own percentiles.

00:02:43.334 --> 00:02:47.588
Bosses are one anonymous group,
there are no verbatim quotes,

00:02:47.588 --> 00:02:50.099
and each leader is an unlabeled dot.

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:52.261
On Northwind's Empowered Teams,

00:02:52.261 --> 00:02:56.307
the dots scatter from three
point six to five point seven.

00:02:56.307 --> 00:03:01.538
As the report says, a tight cluster
reads as a shared strength or weakness,

00:03:01.538 --> 00:03:04.537
and a wide scatter
signals an uneven bench.

00:03:04.537 --> 00:03:07.815
Never rank leaders, or
guess which dot is whom.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.601
The Progress Monitor labels each
domain as meaningful improvement,

00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:15.468
meaningful decline, or
no meaningful change.

00:03:15.468 --> 00:03:20.486
Imagine two different crowds answering
the same questions eight months apart.

00:03:20.486 --> 00:03:25.373
Their averages will almost never be
identical, even if nothing has changed,

00:03:25.373 --> 00:03:28.696
because they're different
people on different days.

00:03:28.696 --> 00:03:33.062
The question is whether the gap is
bigger than that natural wobble.

00:03:33.062 --> 00:03:35.278
That's what Welch's t-test checks.

00:03:35.278 --> 00:03:39.644
It looks at the size of the change,
how spread out the answers are,

00:03:39.644 --> 00:03:42.185
and how many people answered each time.

00:03:42.185 --> 00:03:47.203
If a gap that large would turn up less
than five times in a hundred by chance

00:03:47.203 --> 00:03:52.546
alone, and both waves meet the minimum
group size, the report calls it meaningful.

00:03:52.546 --> 00:03:55.153
Otherwise, it's within normal variation.

00:03:55.153 --> 00:04:00.562
What the test doesn't do is judge whether
the change is big enough to matter to the

00:04:00.562 --> 00:04:01.148
business.

00:04:01.148 --> 00:04:02.191
That's your job.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:08.451
At Northwind, Collaboration improved
meaningfully, from forty-four to fifty-two.

00:04:08.451 --> 00:04:12.836
Change Ready and Empowered Teams
showed no meaningful change.

00:04:12.836 --> 00:04:18.299
That's not the same as "no change", and
definitely not "the program failed".

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:20.024
It can mean four things.

00:04:20.024 --> 00:04:21.174
Nothing changed.

00:04:21.174 --> 00:04:24.625
Something changed, but
too little to detect yet.

00:04:24.625 --> 00:04:27.788
It's too soon, because
culture moves slowly.

00:04:27.788 --> 00:04:32.963
Or there were too few respondents for
the test to pick up a real change.

00:04:32.963 --> 00:04:35.551
The data alone can't tell you which.

00:04:35.551 --> 00:04:40.080
Say so honestly, and suggest what
would tell you: another wave,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.093
or stories that explain why.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.573
The reverse trap exists too.

00:04:44.573 --> 00:04:48.589
A meaningful change isn't
automatically an important one.

00:04:48.589 --> 00:04:53.310
With hundreds of respondents, even
a small shift can pass the test,

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.621
so always say how big
the change is, in points.

00:04:56.621 --> 00:05:01.553
Northwind's plus eight on Collaboration
moves the tile up a size band.

00:05:01.553 --> 00:05:06.697
That's worth celebrating, while being
clear it's still a mid-range score.

00:05:06.697 --> 00:05:11.136
And never say a meaningful change
proves the actions caused it.

00:05:11.136 --> 00:05:16.984
Say "Collaboration improved meaningfully
over the period in which the huddles ran",

00:05:16.984 --> 00:05:21.000
not "the huddles improved
collaboration by eight points".

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.201
Watch the response rate across waves.

00:05:24.201 --> 00:05:28.288
Northwind's fell from sixty-four
percent to forty-seven.

00:05:28.288 --> 00:05:34.127
Each wave is a different, anonymous group,
so if the mix of who answers changes,

00:05:34.127 --> 00:05:37.776
scores can move even when
nobody's experience did.

00:05:37.776 --> 00:05:41.061
Ask which groups answered
less, like Nursing,

00:05:41.061 --> 00:05:45.586
which fell from a hundred and
sixty-one to a hundred and four,

00:05:45.586 --> 00:05:49.089
and whether those people
would feel differently.

00:05:49.089 --> 00:05:53.396
Put this caveat in any briefing
where the rate moved a lot.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:58.349
With narrative tools, always look
at the spread before the average.

00:05:58.349 --> 00:06:03.727
Northwind's Mosaic Signal slider between
official announcements and word of mouth

00:06:03.727 --> 00:06:06.781
has a mean of fifty-two,
which looks balanced.

00:06:06.781 --> 00:06:11.429
But the distribution shows two camps,
about four in ten near each end.

00:06:11.429 --> 00:06:16.541
That's a completely different finding:
two parts of the organization get news

00:06:16.541 --> 00:06:18.334
through different channels.

00:06:18.334 --> 00:06:21.720
Read the shape first: one
hump, two humps, or flat.

00:06:22.233 --> 00:06:28.016
Northwind's change readiness triangle shows
stories bunched along the Willing side,

00:06:28.016 --> 00:06:29.689
with very few near Able.

00:06:29.689 --> 00:06:34.217
Switch between dots and density
to see where stories concentrate,

00:06:34.217 --> 00:06:39.025
then read the stories behind the
cluster before you name the pattern.

00:06:39.025 --> 00:06:42.230
"We were told to use the
new system on Monday.

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:46.550
Nobody had logins until Thursday."
Now the pattern has a name,

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:51.706
and a fix: people are willing, and the
organization hasn't made them able.

00:06:51.706 --> 00:06:55.538
Remember, clusters are what
you see, not a calculation,

00:06:55.538 --> 00:06:57.907
and there's no score or benchmark.

00:06:57.907 --> 00:07:02.227
Don't present a Mosaic Signal
result as "sixty percent ready".

00:07:02.733 --> 00:07:06.236
Culture Dig composites
follow three reading rules.

00:07:06.236 --> 00:07:11.911
Every composite has a ninety-five percent
margin, so read "forty-one point seven,

00:07:11.911 --> 00:07:17.516
plus or minus five point six" as somewhere
from about thirty-six to forty-seven.

00:07:17.516 --> 00:07:20.878
A domain needs ten or more
stories to be scored.

00:07:20.878 --> 00:07:23.120
And it's a pattern, not a grade.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:28.024
A low composite means stories read
as less predictable, more draining,

00:07:28.024 --> 00:07:33.068
or more blame-driven, so look at the
grid and the triangle to see which.

00:07:33.068 --> 00:07:36.781
If two ranges don't overlap,
the difference is clear.

00:07:36.781 --> 00:07:39.514
If they overlap a lot, don't rank them.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.451
A report is evidence.

00:07:41.451 --> 00:07:44.145
A finding is what you conclude from it.

00:07:44.145 --> 00:07:48.705
Write every finding on a Finding
Card before it goes near a slide.

00:07:48.705 --> 00:07:53.749
The claim is one plain sentence: people
at Northwind are clear on why the

00:07:53.749 --> 00:07:57.342
organization exists, but
not how it's getting there.

00:07:57.342 --> 00:08:02.869
The evidence is two or three data points
with their N: Purpose eighty-two versus

00:08:02.869 --> 00:08:08.189
Strategy fifty-one, and leaders seventy-two
versus non-leaders forty-seven on

00:08:08.189 --> 00:08:08.811
Strategy.

00:08:08.811 --> 00:08:11.781
Confidence is high,
medium or low, and why.

00:08:11.781 --> 00:08:16.963
Here it's high: large N, a consistent
pattern, and support in the comments.

00:08:16.963 --> 00:08:21.869
And the so-what: the strategy isn't
being translated for the frontline,

00:08:21.869 --> 00:08:24.356
which is a risk for the integration.

00:08:24.867 --> 00:08:29.802
Your strongest findings come from
different tools pointing the same way.

00:08:29.802 --> 00:08:32.955
Take "decisions feel
closed to the frontline".

00:08:32.955 --> 00:08:36.725
The Culture Mosaic shows
Empowered Teams at forty-nine,

00:08:36.725 --> 00:08:41.935
with a twenty-five-point leader gap and
a comment theme about decisions made

00:08:41.935 --> 00:08:43.580
without frontline input.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:47.144
The COO's 360 shows a blind
spot on Empowered Teams.

00:08:47.144 --> 00:08:52.765
And the progress check shows no meaningful
change, with that comment theme steady.

00:08:52.765 --> 00:08:56.329
Three tools, one pattern:
a high-confidence finding.

00:08:56.329 --> 00:08:57.426
Two disciplines.

00:08:57.426 --> 00:09:02.704
A connection is a hypothesis, not proof,
so put it on the table for dialogue.

00:09:02.704 --> 00:09:04.898
And divergence is a finding too.

00:09:04.898 --> 00:09:09.353
If scores look healthy but stories
say otherwise, report the gap.

00:09:09.867 --> 00:09:14.660
A colleague drafts a finding for the
Northwind board: the Clinic Network has a

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:17.794
culture problem, and the
merger has clearly failed.

00:09:17.794 --> 00:09:19.331
What's the best feedback?

00:09:19.331 --> 00:09:20.499
Choose your answer.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.426
Here are ten traps.

00:09:22.426 --> 00:09:26.704
One: treating a percentile
and a score as the same thing.

00:09:26.704 --> 00:09:28.505
Always name the measure.

00:09:28.505 --> 00:09:32.783
Two: comparing one-to-five
means with one-to-seven means.

00:09:32.783 --> 00:09:36.236
Three: reading the typical
marker as a target.

00:09:36.236 --> 00:09:38.638
Four: over-reading small groups.

00:09:38.638 --> 00:09:42.165
Five: treating no meaningful
change as failure.

00:09:42.165 --> 00:09:45.242
Six: reading only the average on sliders.

00:09:45.242 --> 00:09:48.395
Seven: ranking leaders from a Team Report.

00:09:48.395 --> 00:09:51.247
Eight: hunting for who said a comment,

00:09:51.247 --> 00:09:54.699
and stopping others from
doing it in the room.

00:09:54.699 --> 00:09:58.677
Nine: making causal claims
from Links to Performance.

00:09:58.677 --> 00:10:03.255
And ten: ignoring the response
rate, especially across waves.

00:10:03.255 --> 00:10:06.558
Open each one to see an
example and the fix.

00:10:07.067 --> 00:10:11.312
There are three lines you never
cross, whatever the client asks.

00:10:11.312 --> 00:10:16.552
Never try to identify a respondent: not
from a comment, not from a small group,

00:10:16.552 --> 00:10:20.266
and not by subtracting one
group's score from another's.

00:10:20.266 --> 00:10:24.047
Never present a group the
report suppressed or rolled up,

00:10:24.047 --> 00:10:26.833
like Northwind's two-person Pharmacy team.

00:10:26.833 --> 00:10:30.481
And never overclaim: no
"validated to predict returns",

00:10:30.481 --> 00:10:34.726
no "proves the program worked",
no "this leader is the problem".

00:10:34.726 --> 00:10:37.578
These are critical
items in the final exam.

00:10:38.067 --> 00:10:40.278
Now practise with real exhibits.

00:10:40.278 --> 00:10:45.670
In Case A, you'll use Northwind's Culture
Mosaic to write three Finding Cards,

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:50.162
name one thing you'd be careful
not to say to the executive team,

00:10:50.162 --> 00:10:52.720
and state the "Where to start" tiles.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:55.830
Then compare your work
with the model answer.

00:10:56.333 --> 00:10:59.167
In Case B, you'll read Jordan Reyes's 360.

00:10:59.167 --> 00:11:02.473
Identify two blind spots
and one hidden strength,

00:11:02.473 --> 00:11:08.074
describe one rater-group disagreement,
explain why the "Other" raters don't appear,

00:11:08.074 --> 00:11:12.190
and choose the development theme
you'd open the debrief with.

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:16.278
In Case C, you'll read
Northwind's progress check.

00:11:16.278 --> 00:11:21.787
Describe each domain using the report's
language correctly, name two caveats,

00:11:21.787 --> 00:11:27.511
answer the CEO's question "so did the
huddles work?", and recommend a next step.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:33.632
And in Case D, a different client: Harbor
and Pine Credit Union's Culture Dig.

00:11:33.632 --> 00:11:38.758
Decide which differences between
domains you can state with confidence,

00:11:38.758 --> 00:11:43.307
what the report can say about a
domain with only eight stories,

00:11:43.307 --> 00:11:47.278
and how to respond when the
sponsor says HR is failing.

00:11:47.767 --> 00:11:52.221
Time for the module check: fifteen
questions, and you need twelve right.

00:11:52.221 --> 00:11:56.860
Retakes are unlimited, and you'll be
routed to the topics behind any missed

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:57.850
questions first.

00:11:58.333 --> 00:12:02.095
Here's your one-page guide to
reading the Culture Mosaic report.

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:05.761
Here's the same for the Leadership
Mosaic and the Team Report.

00:12:06.267 --> 00:12:09.271
And one for reading the
Culture Progress Monitor.

00:12:09.767 --> 00:12:11.563
Here's a blank Finding Card.

00:12:11.563 --> 00:12:14.834
Fill in one per finding,
and aim for three to five.

00:12:15.333 --> 00:12:18.050
And the ten interpretation
traps on one card.

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:20.767
Keep it beside you when
you build a briefing.

00:12:21.267 --> 00:12:23.302
Here's what you can now do.

00:12:23.302 --> 00:12:29.182
Name the measure every time: item mean,
zero-to-one-hundred score, percentile,

00:12:29.182 --> 00:12:30.011
or typical.

00:12:30.011 --> 00:12:34.911
Check N, response rate and
suppression before you trust a number.

00:12:34.911 --> 00:12:39.961
Find blind spots, hidden strengths
and rater disagreement yourself.

00:12:39.961 --> 00:12:43.730
Describe change honestly:
meaningful isn't causal,

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:46.670
and no meaningful change isn't failure.

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:52.625
Read narrative data shape-first, and
treat composites as patterns with margins.

00:12:52.625 --> 00:12:57.449
And turn evidence into Finding
Cards, triangulated across tools.

00:12:57.449 --> 00:13:00.163
Next: Module 9, briefing executives.

